---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PolicySection from "../components/Privacy/PolicySection.astro";

const lastUpdated = "November 2026";

const shortVersion = [
  {
    claim: "No cookies.",
    aside: "Not even the edible kind. We checked the whole site twice.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No tracking.",
    aside:
      "Which also means we have no idea whether anyone reads this. Hi, if you do.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No ads.",
    aside: "Nobody is bidding on your attention here.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No accounts.",
    aside: "Neither here nor in Rhymate. One less password to forget.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — digitowly"
  description="How digitowly and Rhymate handle your data. Short version: as little as possible."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="gutter relative overflow-hidden bg-paper pt-36 pb-16 sm:pt-44 sm:pb-24">
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="pointer-events-none absolute -top-[25vw] -left-[30vw] size-[80vw] rounded-full bg-mist/70 blur-3xl"
    ></div>
    <p class="rise relative text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] text-moss uppercase">
      Privacy
    </p>
    <h1
      class="display rise relative mt-6 text-huge text-balance"
      style="animation-delay: 120ms"
    >
      Your data is{" "}
      <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal text-moss">yours.</span>
      We’d like to keep it that way.
    </h1>
    <p
      class="rise relative mt-10 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/75"
      style="animation-delay: 260ms"
    >
      What happens to your data when you visit this website or use Rhymate. The
      short version fits on a postcard. The long version exists because the law
      likes paragraphs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Short version -->
  <section class="gutter bg-sage py-20 text-moss sm:py-32">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] uppercase">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-10">
      {shortVersion.map(({ claim, aside }) => (
        <li class="flex flex-col gap-2 border-t border-moss/25 py-7 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:justify-between sm:gap-10">
          <span class="display text-big">{claim}</span>
          <span class="serif-accent max-w-md text-xl leading-snug text-moss/80 sm:text-right sm:text-2xl">
            {aside}
          </span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Why -->
  <section class="gutter bg-sand py-20 sm:py-32">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-20">
      <h2 class="display text-big text-balance">
        Privacy is part of{" "}
        <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal">good UX</span>. Not a
        checkbox next to it.
      </h2>
      <div class="max-w-xl space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/80">
        <p>
          We think software should feel calm. That's hard to pull off while it's
          quietly reporting your every tap to a dozen companies in the
          background. Cookie banners, trackers and “we value your privacy”
          pop-ups are a bad experience on their own, before you even get to the
          data part.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we skip all of it. It's honestly less work for us, and we promise
          the laziness is principled.
        </p>
        <p>
          When something does need your data, like looking up a rhyme, we send
          only what the job requires and say so below.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Long version -->
  <section class="gutter bg-paper py-20 sm:py-32">
    <div class="mb-6 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-end sm:justify-between">
      <h2 class="display text-big">The long version</h2>
      <p class="serif-accent text-xl text-ink/60">
        The part with the paragraphs. Last updated {lastUpdated}.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PolicySection no="01" title="Who is responsible">
      <p>
        The controller responsible for data processing on this website and in
        Rhymate within the meaning of the GDPR is:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>digitowly</strong> · Robert Pasdziernik
        <br />
        Düsseldorfer Straße 159, 51063 Köln, Germany
        <br />
        <a href="mailto:info@digitowly.com">info@digitowly.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        More details are in the <a href="/impressum">Impressum</a>. If you have
        any questions about privacy, just write. A real human will answer.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="02" title="Visiting this website">
      <p>
        This website is hosted by <strong>Netlify, Inc.</strong> (San Francisco,
        USA). Like any web server, theirs needs some technical information to
        deliver a page to you. When you visit, the following is processed
        automatically and may be stored in server log files for a short time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your IP address</li>
        <li>date and time of the request</li>
        <li>the page requested and the referring page</li>
        <li>browser type, version and operating system</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This data is used solely to deliver the website and keep it secure and
        stable. It is not combined with other data and not used to build
        profiles. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest
        in running a working, secure website. Netlify may process this data in
        the USA. More information is in{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Netlify's privacy policy
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="03" title="Cookies, analytics & fonts">
      <p>
        This website does <strong>not</strong> use cookies, analytics, tracking
        pixels, social media plugins or advertising. There is nothing to consent
        to, which is why you didn't have to dismiss a banner to read this.
      </p>
      <p>
        All fonts are hosted on our own server. Your browser doesn't contact
        Google Fonts or any other third-party service when it loads this site.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="04" title="Contacting us">
      <p>
        If you send us an email, we use your address and whatever you write to
        answer you and, if needed, follow up. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b)
        GDPR if your message is about a project or contract, otherwise Art.
        6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest in replying to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        We delete the conversation once it's done, unless legal retention
        periods (for example for invoices) require us to keep it longer.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="05" title="Rhymate">
      <p>
        Rhymate has no accounts, no analytics and no ads. We don't run a server
        that collects anything from the app. Here is what it does with data:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your lyrics and favourites</strong> are stored on your device.
          If you use iCloud, they sync through your private iCloud database at
          Apple. We can't see that data, and neither can anyone else except you
          (and Apple, under their{" "}
          <a
            href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            privacy policy
          </a>
          ).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rhyme and synonym searches.</strong> When you look up a word,
          that word is sent to the{" "}
          <a
            href="https://www.datamuse.com/api/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Datamuse API
          </a>{" "}
          to find matches. Only the word is sent, never your lyrics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Definitions.</strong> When you open a word's definition, that
          word is sent to{" "}
          <a
            href="https://en.wiktionary.org/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Wiktionary
          </a>
          , run by the Wikimedia Foundation (
          <a
            href="https://foundation.wikimedia.org/wiki/Policy:Privacy_policy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            privacy policy
          </a>
          ).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        As with any internet request, these services receive your IP address
        technically. Both are based in the USA. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b)
        GDPR: without sending the word, we can't find rhymes for it. (We tried
        guessing. Results were poor.)
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="06" title="Your rights">
      <p>Under the GDPR, you have the right to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>access the personal data we hold about you (Art. 15)</li>
        <li>have it corrected (Art. 16) or deleted (Art. 17)</li>
        <li>restrict its processing (Art. 18)</li>
        <li>receive it in a portable format (Art. 20)</li>
        <li>object to processing based on legitimate interest (Art. 21)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To use any of these, email{" "}
        <a href="mailto:info@digitowly.com">info@digitowly.com</a>. Given how
        little we store, it'll be a short email.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory
        authority. For us, that is the Landesbeauftragte für Datenschutz und
        Informationsfreiheit Nordrhein-Westfalen (
        <a
          href="https://www.ldi.nrw.de/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          ldi.nrw.de
        </a>
        ).
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="07" title="Changes">
      <p>
        If we change how we handle data, for example when Rhymate gets a new
        feature, we'll update this page. The “last updated” date above tells you
        when that last happened.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>
  </section>
</Layout>
